<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Configuration>

  <!--
    Start a packaged Papyrus install inside the Windows Sandbox and leave it running, so a person
    can actually use it.

    ## Why this exists alongside papyrus.wsb

    Because a gate and a demo are opposites, and running one to get the other does not work.

    `run-in-sandbox.ps1` starts the install, tests it, stops it, and reports. Nothing is left behind,
    which is exactly right for a gate and exactly wrong for someone who wants to look at the thing.
    The obvious workaround, launching the gate and interrupting it, leaves the app in whatever state
    the test had reached, which is not the state a reader meets: half its processes up, a data
    directory built by a test rather than a person, and no way to know which of those were meant to
    be true.

    So this is a second runner with a second job: same setup, then hold.

    ## The ports are the defaults, and that is deliberate

    8000 and 4567, which is what a real install uses, rather than the 8402 and 4571 the gate forces.
    Somebody looking at this should be seeing the addresses a reader would actually see.

    This matters more than it sounds. The whole reason this file was written is that a healthy
    install was reached for at the wrong port twice, and both times the only evidence on screen was
    ERR_CONNECTION_REFUSED. Using the gate's ports here would bake that confusion into the one place
    somebody is being shown how to use the app.

    ## Where the install goes

    C:\Program Files\Papyrus. That is where a Windows installer puts one, and it is the awkward path
    with a space in it, which is half of what the sandbox gate exists to catch.

    ## It does not prompt, and it does not ask for anything

    If Papyrus fails to start, the reason goes to C:\results\hold-open.txt on the host, and the
    terminal stays open so it can also be read on screen. There is no prompt of any kind, because a
    prompt inside a sandbox either cannot be answered or gets answered badly, and both look like a
    hang.

    ## One guest at a time, and the results folder must exist first

    Both inherited from papyrus.wsb, and both cost real time there. Windows Sandbox refuses to start
    a second guest while one is open, saying so in a window nobody is watching, and it will not
    create a missing mapped folder: the mount fails, the logon command never runs, and the sandbox
    closes looking exactly like a slow boot.
  -->

  <VGpu>Disable</VGpu>
  <Networking>Default</Networking>

  <MappedFolders>
    <MappedFolder>
      <HostFolder>%PAPYRUS_REPO_ROOT%\build</HostFolder>
      <SandboxFolder>C:\build</SandboxFolder>
      <ReadOnly>true</ReadOnly>
    </MappedFolder>
    <MappedFolder>
      <HostFolder>%PAPYRUS_REPO_ROOT%\build\sandbox-results</HostFolder>
      <SandboxFolder>C:\results</SandboxFolder>
      <ReadOnly>false</ReadOnly>
    </MappedFolder>
  </MappedFolders>

  <LogonCommand>
    <Command>powershell.exe -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File C:\build\sandbox\hold-open.ps1</Command>
  </LogonCommand>

</Configuration>